    {
      "slug": "reddit-laundry-room-spreadsheet",
      "script": "Our apartment building had exactly two washers for forty units, and one neighbor kept leaving her clothes inside for hours. Management ignored every complaint. So I started logging timestamps instead: load time, cycle finish, and how long the wet clothes sat. After three weeks, the spreadsheet was airtight. I posted it anonymously in the building group chat and let the other thirty-nine tenants connect the dots. Within a week, management installed a sign-up sheet with strict thirty-minute pickup windows and a small fine. She was the first person fined, three days later, then called the rule unfair to the one person it was obviously written for. I still do laundry at midnight most weeks, but now I always get a machine. The spin cycle needed receipts.",
      "editor_note": "Compressed the story to 126 words, preserved every protected fact and visual phrase, and ended on a laundry-specific receipts callback."
    },
    {
      "slug": "reddit-freelance-license-revoked",
      "script": "I designed a full brand package for a startup founder who ghosted on the final invoice, then used my logo for two months while calling the work incomplete. My contract had one clause he missed: unpaid work reverts to a revocable license, and nonpayment past thirty days triggers automatic revocation. I sent one email confirming the lapse, then filed infringement reports directly with his web host and payment processor, attaching timestamped proof. His site went dark within a week, while he was running paid ads for a product launch teased for a month. He paid the full invoice plus a reinstatement fee for my written release—more than double what he owed. No apology, just a wire transfer and a new logo order from someone with better sense. The contract did excellent follow-up.",
      "editor_note": "Cut the story to 132 words, preserved every protected fact and visual phrase, and sharpened the payoff around the contract enforcing itself."
    }
  ],
